"Compares treatment responses within nuisance-factor blocks to see whether a treatment effect persists across conditions.\n\nUse when: An experiment uses blocks to account for nuisance variation. Treatment consistency across block conditions matters more than an overall mean alone.\n\nAvoid when: Block identities are arbitrary or treatment comparisons are missing for most blocks.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position identifies a block or nuisance-factor combination. Vertical position encodes response, with distinct symbols or a within-block link identifying treatments.\n\nBuild: Define blocks from the experimental design rather than observed outcomes. Compute the chosen treatment response within each block. Place treatment marks together within each block with short block labels outside the data region.\n\nCheck: Check treatments are matched to the correct blocks. Verify comparable response units and aggregation across all blocks. Identify missing treatment-block combinations without inventing values.\n\nPitfalls: Unbalanced replication can make summary comparisons misleading unless weights are declared. Do not connect across unrelated blocks; keep any paired connectors within the block’s own visual slot.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic adhesive performance for two formulations within each temperature-and-batch block to inspect consistency of the formulation difference.\n\nAlternatives: Interaction plot, Dumbbell chart, Main effects plot."
